\section{Forward a Load Byte by Byte Through Overlapping Stores}

Partial overlap need not defeat store-to-load forwarding if the exact accessed bytes are known. Consider a small sequential IR with a chain of nonvolatile, nonfaulting stores to statically known byte intervals and a load with a fixed address and width. Assume initialized memory and a known target byte order. Stores contribute the encoded bytes of their already evaluated values; none is removed by this transformation. The input is the load and its incoming memory version. The pass either rewrites the load to a value assembled from dominating store operands or declines when it cannot identify the origin of every byte.

\begin{verbatim}
forward_bytes(addr, width, memory):
  need = addresses(addr, width); byte = empty map
  while need is not empty:
    if memory is not KnownStore: return KeepLoad
    s = store_def(memory)
    for a in need intersect s.interval:
      # A later store already won for any removed address.
      byte[a] = encoded_byte(s.value, a - s.start)
      remove a from need
    memory = s.previous
  # Address order and integer significance are distinct.
  return assemble(byte in address order, load_endianness)
\end{verbatim}

On a little-endian target a two-byte store of hexadecimal \texttt{1234} at address \(100\) writes \texttt{34} and \texttt{12}. A subsequent one-byte store of \texttt{AB} at \(101\) sets that byte to \texttt{AB}. For a two-byte load at \(100\), the reverse walk takes byte \(101\) from the later store and byte \(100\) from the earlier store. Assembly returns hexadecimal \texttt{AB34}. Forwarding the first store's whole value would instead return \texttt{1234}. A store of unknown footprint between those two stores prevents this particular pass from finding a source for byte \(100\).

The byte provenance in Figure~\ref{fig:visual-194} makes the difference between address order and numeric significance explicit.

